# Do markets work? Putting it together
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Efficient, fair, innovative: three answers When markets work, when they fail, and when the cure is worse"
next: {"title":"Test: do markets work?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/70-test-do-markets-work.cast"}

## One vaccine, three grades
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Low income
      - Lower-middle
      - Upper-middle
      - High income
    values:
      - 8
      - 44
      - 74
      - 72
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: At least one dose (%)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 110
      w: 600
      h: 540
sources:
    - id: owid_vax
      title: Share of people with at least one dose of COVID-19 vaccine, by World Bank income group
      authors: Our World in Data (Mathieu et al.)
      year: 2024
      finding: '31 Dec 2021: low-income countries 8.0%, lower-middle 44.3%, upper-middle 73.7%, high-income 72.3%.'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/share-people-vaccinated-covid
    - id: mhra2020
      title: Pfizer and BioNTech achieve first authorization in the world for a vaccine to combat COVID-19
      authors: Pfizer / BioNTech press release
      year: 2020
      finding: UK MHRA granted temporary emergency authorisation on 2 December 2020; the SARS-CoV-2 genome had been posted on 10-11 January 2020.
    - id: ows2020
      title: Pfizer and BioNTech announce an agreement with U.S. government for up to 600 million doses
      authors: Pfizer press release
      year: 2020
      finding: '22 July 2020: the US government to pay $1.95 billion for the first 100 million doses, on authorisation, while the vaccine was still in phase 1/2 trials.'

    text date "by 31 Dec 2021" #8f887c cites ["owid_vax"] x 480 y 640 font_size 24 hidden true
    text t1 "Jan 2020: virus code" cites ["mhra2020"] x 350 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text t2 "Dec 2020: first vaccine" cites ["mhra2020"] x 350 y 410 font_size 34 hidden true
    text b1 "US: $1.95bn, 100m doses" cites ["ows2020"] x 350 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text b2 "before the big trial" x 350 y 410 font_size 34 hidden true
    box g_inn "Innovative: A" #2e7d4f x 820 y 510 width 340 height 95 radius 12 icon "light bulb" font_size 32 hidden true
    box g_fair "Fair: F (values)" #b5482e x 820 y 385 width 340 height 95 radius 12 icon "balance scale" font_size 32 hidden true
    box g_eff "Efficient: ?" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 260 width 340 height 95 radius 12 icon syringe font_size 32 hidden true
    text e1 "spillover" #8a5fa8 x 740 y 190 font_size 26 hidden true
    text e2 "patent" #8a5fa8 x 900 y 190 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "One vaccine, three grades"

Do markets work? Here's one market that puts the block's three tests to work at once: the Covid vaccines.
    draw axes date

B: The virus's genetic (@draw t1@) code was published in January 2020. In December, eleven month (@draw t2@)s later, Britain approved the first vaccine.

On the innovative te (@draw g_inn@)st, that's top marks. Firms raced for a prize, and the prize paid for the race.

    erase t1 t2

B: With help. In July 2020 the US agreed to  (@draw b1@)pay nearly two billion dollars for a hundred million doses, before th (@draw b2@)e big trial had even begun.

    erase b1 b2

Now the fair test. By the end of 2021, about seve (@draw bar_4 bar_3 bar_2@)n in ten people in high and upper-middle-income countries had a first dose, and forty-four percent in lower-middle.

    ask "Drag the 'Low income' bar to your guess: what share had a first dose by the end of 2021?" on bar_1 store g default "40" voice b
        intro: And in the poorest countries? Drag the bar to your guess.
        right: Close: about eight percent.
        wrong: Lower still: about eight percent.

About eight percent. One person in twelve. On fairness, by most people's standard, a f (@draw g_fair ends@)ail.
    highlight bar_1 effect circle

B: Efficient  (@draw g_eff@)is the tangled grade. A shot also protects the peopl (@draw e1@)e around you, a benefit no buyer pays for.

And a patent keeps th (@draw e2@)e price above what one more dose (@highlight g_eff@) costs to make: a small monopoly, granted on purpose.

So one market earns three grades at once. Asking whether markets work never had one answer.
    highlight g_inn g_fair g_eff

    pause 0.5

## The map
    box root "Efficient if…" x 135 y 395 width 230 height 140 radius 12 icon handshake font_size 36 hidden true
    text c1 "many sellers" #8f887c x 135 y 285 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c2 "all costs priced" #8f887c x 135 y 250 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c3 "all informed" #8f887c x 135 y 215 font_size 28 hidden true
    box f1 "Market power" #b5482e x 470 y 560 width 270 height 130 radius 12 icon crown font_size 36 hidden true
    box f2 "Spillovers" #b5482e x 470 y 395 width 270 height 130 radius 12 icon factory font_size 36 hidden true
    box f3 "Hidden info" #b5482e x 470 y 230 width 270 height 130 radius 12 icon "magnifying glass" font_size 36 hidden true
    box r1 "Antitrust\nregulated price" #2f6b8f x 820 y 560 width 320 height 130 radius 12 font_size 34 hidden true
    box r2 "Tax · quota\nrights · own rules" #2f6b8f x 820 y 395 width 320 height 130 radius 12 font_size 34 hidden true
    box r3 "Warranty · reviews\nmandate" #2f6b8f x 820 y 230 width 320 height 130 radius 12 font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow a1 root -> f1 hidden true
    arrow b1 f1 -> r1 hidden true
    arrow a2 root -> f2 hidden true
    arrow b2 f2 -> r2 hidden true
    arrow a3 root -> f3 hidden true
    arrow b3 f3 -> r3 hidden true
    group map members ["root","c1","c2","c3","f1","f2","f3","r1","r2","r3","a1","a2","a3","b1","b2","b3"] hidden true
    icon vax size 70 set twemoji x 135 y 560 icon_look picture of syringe hidden true

    card title "The map"

Three grades from one market. To see where grades li (@draw root@)ke that come from, put the whole block on one page.

B: Start where the welfare theorem starts. Man (@draw c1@)y small sellers, every cost on the p (@draw c2@)rice, everyone knowing what t (@draw c3@)hey buy. Then a market is efficient.

Break the first condition and y (@draw a1 f1@)ou get market power: the monopolist selling less at a higher price, the cartel, the platform.

B: Break the second and you ge (@draw a2 f2@)t spillovers: the smoke, the lighthouse nobody pays for, the overfished grounds.

And break the third and you get (@draw a3 f3@) hidden information: lemons, insurance that unravels, drivers who get careless once covered.

B: Each row has its remedies (@draw b1 r1@). For power: antitrust, or a regulated price.

For spill (@draw b2 r2@)overs: a tax on the harm, a quota, clear property rights, or the users' own rules.

B: For hidden information: warra (@draw b3 r3@)nties and reviews from the market itself, mandates from the state.

The va (@draw vax@)ccine sits on two rows at once: a spi (@highlight f2 effect box@)llover the state paid to fix, and a patent (@highlight f1 effect box@) the law hands out to buy innovation.

    erase vax

    hide map

B: Your turn. Six cases this course hasn't met: sort each onto its row.
    cards sortq check each
        Market power: Lone cement plant
        Spillovers: Overused antibiotics
        Hidden info: Pet cover
        Spillovers: 7 a.m. leaf blowers
        Market power: Valley's only cinema
        Hidden info: Mechanic's 'new gearbox!'

    ask "Which row of the map does each case belong on? Sort the six cards." on sortq store srt stage own voice b
        right: Power: the cement plant and the lone cinema. Spillovers: antibiotics and leaf blowers. Hidden info: the pet cover and the mechanic.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} on the first try. The rows: the cement plant and the lone cinema lack a rival; antibiotics and leaf blowers put an unpriced cost on others; the pet cover and the mechanic turn on what one side knows.

    erase sortq

    show map

Spotting the failure is the easy half. Eve (@highlight b1 b2 b3@)ry arrow from failure to remedy has to be drawn by someone real.

    pause 0.4

## When the cure is worse
domain: {"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10.8]}
sources:
    - id: leape2006
      title: The London Congestion Charge
      authors: Jonathan Leape
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Journal of Economic Perspectives 20(4): delays inside the charging zone fell by about 30% in the first year.'
    - id: davis2008
      title: The Effect of Driving Restrictions on Air Quality in Mexico City
      authors: Lucas W. Davis
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 116(1): no evidence that Hoy No Circula improved air quality; it led to more vehicles in circulation.'
    - id: carbon2019
      title: Economists' Statement on Carbon Dividends
      authors: Climate Leadership Council
      year: 2019
      finding: 'Published in the Wall Street Journal, January 2019; signed by over 3,500 US economists including 27 Nobel laureates: a carbon tax is the most cost-effective lever to cut emissions.'
    - id: dmq2019
      title: 'The Effects of Rent Control Expansion on Tenants, Landlords, and Inequality: Evidence from San Francisco'
      authors: Rebecca Diamond, Tim McQuade, Franklin Qian
      year: 2019
      finding: Landlords subject to the 1994 expansion of rent control cut the supply of rental housing they offered by 15%; sitting tenants gained.
      url: https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.20181289
    - id: igm2012rent
      title: 'IGM Forum: Rent Control (February 2012)'
      authors: IGM Economic Experts Panel
      year: 2012
      finding: Asked whether rent limits in New York and San Francisco had improved the amount and quality of affordable rental housing, only about 2% of the panel agreed; the large majority disagreed.
      url: https://www.kentclarkcenter.org/surveys/rent-control/

    axes ax ticks.x [] ticks.y [] hidden true
    text lab_x "Market failure →" #3d3833 at.data [8.3,-0.7] font_size 34 hidden true
    text lab_y "↑ Government failure" #3d3833 at.data [1.6,10.6] font_size 34 hidden true
