    region reg_worse x_from 0 x_to 10 between ["diag","ceil"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.2 hidden true
    text t_step "Step in" #4f7a43 at.data [7.8,1.2] font_size 60 hidden true
    text t_worse "Cure worse" #b5482e at.data [2.4,8.8] font_size 60 hidden true
    text t_be "break-even" #3d3833 at.data [8.2,9.4] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch why at.data [2.6,6] work ["knows too little","wants the wrong thing"] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot p_lon #2f6b8f at.x 6 at.y 3 cites ["leape2006"] radius 11 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    text p_lon_l "London charge" #2f6b8f at.data [7.7,3] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot p_carb #2f6b8f at.x 9 at.y 4.6 cites ["carbon2019"] radius 11 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    text p_carb_l "Carbon" #2f6b8f at.data [8,4.6] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot p_mex #b5482e at.x 5 at.y 7 cites ["davis2008"] radius 11 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text p_mex_l "Mexico City ban" #b5482e at.data [3.1,7] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot p_rent #b5482e at.x 7 at.y 8.6 cites ["dmq2019","igm2012rent"] radius 11 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    text p_rent_l "Rent cap" #b5482e at.data [5.75,8.6] font_size 40 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch calc center work [{"tex":"\\text{Step in only if } s \\times \\text{loss} > \\text{mistakes}"},{"tex":"s = \\tfrac12,\\ \\text{loss} = 40 \\Rightarrow 20"},{"tex":"\\text{mistakes: } 15 \\Rightarrow \\text{in};\\ 25 \\Rightarrow \\text{out}"}] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "When the cure is worse"

Every remedy (@draw ax@) on that map needs a fixer: a council, a regulator, a parliament. So weigh the failure against the fixer.

B: Along this  (@draw lab_x@)axis, how big the market failure is. Up this one, h (@draw lab_y@)ow badly the real state is likely to botch the fix.

Big f (@draw diag t_be@)ailure, de (@draw reg_step t_step@)cent fixer: step in. Fixer worse tha (@draw reg_worse t_worse@)n the failure: the cure is worse than the disease.

B: And a state fails in two ways. It may know  (@draw why_box why_line_1@)too little: the right size of a carbon tax, what a tonne of smoke really costs.

Or it may  (@draw why_line_2@)want the wrong thing: voters too busy to watch, dentists sitting on the dentists' board.

    erase why

B: Now the block's cases.  (@draw p_lon p_lon_l@)London's congestion charge: a real jam, and a  (@highlight p_lon effect pulse@)charge that cut delays.

And carbon? A huge fail (@draw p_carb p_carb_l@)ure, and a price we'd set imperfectly. Most economists still put it (@highlight p_carb effect circle@) well inside 'step in'.

One case sits up here alre (@draw p_mex p_mex_l@)ady: Mexico City's driving ban. Real smog, but families bought second cars, and the air got no cleaner.

My guess for a rent cap in a city with a real housing shortage:  (@highlight t_step effect circle@)the shortage is real, so surely 'step in'.

    ask "Where does a rent cap in a city short of homes land? Tap 'Step in' or 'Cure worse'." intro "Where would you put it?" choose ["t_step","t_worse"] answer t_worse stage own voice b
        right: Cure worse: a cap treats the high rent, not the shortage, and owners let and build less.
        wrong: The shortage is real. Does capping rents add homes? It tends to cut them: here, cure worse.

B: Above the l (@draw p_rent p_rent_l@)ine, for most economists. Sitting tenants gain, but owners let and build less, so the shortage grows.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own

@math

    erase mathq

    fade ax lab_x lab_y diag t_be reg_step t_step reg_worse t_worse p_lon p_lon_l p_carb p_carb_l p_mex p_mex_l p_rent p_rent_l to 0.12

Step in only if the share of the loss the state really removes, times the loss, beats the cost of its own mistakes.
    draw calc_box calc_line_1

B: Say the  (@draw calc_line_2@)failure wastes forty a year, and a real state removes half of it: twenty.

(@draw calc_line_3@) If its mistakes cost fifteen, step in. If they cost twenty-five, leave it.

    erase calc

@after

    erase mathq

    fade ax lab_x lab_y diag t_be reg_step t_step reg_worse t_worse p_lon p_lon_l p_carb p_carb_l p_mex p_mex_l p_rent p_rent_l to 1

So the diagonal is the whol (@highlight diag@)e question of when the cure is worse, and it's drawn case by case, with evidence.

    pause 0.4

## Three toolkits, one verdict
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: 'Market, state or both: you read the map like an economist.'
    good: Ask what fails, then who would fix it.
    poor: 'Two things can fail in every case: the market and the fixer.'
    none: The map will wait for you.
sources:
    - id: ostrom1990
      title: Governing the Commons
      authors: Elinor Ostrom
      year: 1990
      finding: Many communities manage shared resources (fisheries, pastures, irrigation) with their own rules, without either private ownership or state control.
    - id: gneezy2000
      title: A Fine Is a Price
      authors: Uri Gneezy, Aldo Rustichini
      year: 2000
      finding: 'Journal of Legal Studies 29(1): a fine for late pickup in Haifa day-care centres increased the number of late-coming parents; removing it did not reverse the rise.'

    box k1 "Equilibrium:\nwhere's the triangle?" #3d3833 x 170 y 420 width 260 height 250 radius 12 icon "balance scale" font_size 30 hidden true
    box k2 "Institutions:\nwho sets the rules?" #2f6b8f cites ["ostrom1990"] x 500 y 420 width 260 height 250 radius 12 icon "classical building" font_size 30 hidden true
    box k3 "Behaviour: does\na price change us?" #8a5fa8 cites ["gneezy2000"] x 830 y 420 width 260 height 250 radius 12 icon brain font_size 30 hidden true
    text c1 "Efficient if the three hold" x 370 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s1 "consensus" #2e7d4f x 820 y 610 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c2 "Real markets break them" x 370 y 500 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s2 "consensus" #2e7d4f x 820 y 500 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c3 "This cure beats this failure" x 370 y 390 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s3 "contested" #b5482e x 820 y 390 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c4 "How to weigh the three tests" x 370 y 280 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s4 "open" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 280 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c5 "Stable?" #8f887c x 370 y 175 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s5 "later" #8f887c x 820 y 175 font_size 44 hidden true
    group ladder members ["c1","c2","c3","c4","s1","s2","s3","s4"] hidden true

    card title "Three toolkits, one verdict"

Case by case, then. But notice whose map t (@draw k1@)his is: the equilibrium economist's, with fixed wants and prices that go wrong.

B: Other lectures explained things differ (@draw k2@)ently. Ostrom's fishers kept their grounds with their own  (@highlight k2@)rules: neither market nor state.

And when the Haifa day-car (@draw k3@)es fined parents for coming late, more parents came late. A price can change the peopl (@highlight k3@)e it means to steer.

B: So the map's two exits, market o (@fade k1 to 0.3@)r state, are too few, and a rem (@highlight k2 k3@)edy can backfire on the people it targets.

So how sur (@draw c1 c2 c3 c4 parallel true@)e are we?
    erase k1 k2 k3

B: That a market meeting the three conditions is eff (@draw s1@)icient: consensus. That real markets break them, in just the ways on the map:  (@draw s2 ends@)consensus too.

Whether a given cure beat (@draw s3@)s a given failure: contested, and settled only by evidence.

B: And how to weigh efficient against  (@draw s4@)fair against innovative: open. That part is values, not economics.

So, do markets work? At slicing t (@highlight ladder@)oday's pie, often; at sharing it, unevenly; at growing it, remarkably. Where they fail, the test is the state we would really get.

    quiz "Say a ride-hailing app's fares jump fivefold in a storm. A critic calls it gouging; a defender says twice as many drivers come out. Which tests are they using?" voice b
        intro: Three new cases to try the map on.
        right: Fair against efficient: the critic asks who pays, the defender how many rides the high price brings out.
        wrong: One talks about who pays, the other about how many rides happen: here, fair against efficient.
        + Fair against efficient
        * Innovative against stable
        * Both use efficiency

    quiz "Upstream farmers drain a river that fishers downstream depend on. The council that would set water quotas is mostly farmers. What must be checked before it acts?"
        right: Whether this council would do better than none: the spillover is real, but a farmers' council is a capture risk.
        wrong: Two things can fail here, not one: here, check whether this council would do better than none.
        + Whether a council like this would do better than none
        * Whether fishers are poorer than farmers
        * Nothing: a spillover always justifies quotas

    quiz "A town starts paying its library volunteers five dollars an hour. Which toolkit warns that fewer of them might turn up?" voice b
        right: Behaviour and norms: a small payment can turn a good deed into a badly paid job, and some helpers stop coming.
        wrong: Is the worry a price set wrong, or what a small price does to why people help? The second: here, behaviour and norms.
        + Behaviour and norms
        * Equilibrium: find the triangle
        * Market power

Whether they stay steady thro (@draw c5 s5@)ugh booms and busts is the question this course has saved for later.

    pause 0.5

